\begin{abstract}
Dense temporal stacks of commercial X-band SAR imagery offer fine-grained surface-deformation monitoring, yet phase quality degrades sharply when acquisitions span a wide range of incidence angles and orbital geometries - a common scenario in Capella Space constellation stacks.  Classical Goldstein filtering ignores this geometry diversity, yielding inconsistent interferograms and high triplet-closure errors.  We propose a \textbf{FiLM-conditioned U-Net} (FiLMUNet) that denoises wrapped complex interferograms while explicitly conditioning on a seven-dimensional geometry vector $[\Delta t,\,\theta_{\rm inc},\,\theta_{\rm graze},\,B_\perp,\,{\rm mode},\,{\rm look},\,{\rm SNR}]$.  Training is entirely self-supervised: a Noise2Noise objective on frequency-domain sub-look splits is augmented with triplet-closure consistency and SBAS temporal residual losses - no clean reference interferograms are required. The model also predicts per-pixel aleatoric uncertainty, which propagates downstream to replace uniform weights in SNAPHU phase unwrapping and SBAS stack inversion.  Applied to the 221-collect Hawaii stack from the IEEE GRSS 2026 Data Fusion Contest (AOI\_000), FiLMUNet reduces triplet closure error by [XX]\% and temporal residual by {[XX]} \% over Goldstein, with the full pipeline publicly reproducible from open S3 data.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
InSAR, phase denoising, self-supervised learning, Noise2Noise,
FiLM conditioning, SBAS, Capella Space, phase closure
\end{IEEEkeywords}

% -----------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Introduction}

Repeat-pass interferometric SAR (InSAR) enables millimeter-scale surface-deformation monitoring from spaceborne platforms, but interferogram phase quality is a fundamental bottleneck: low coherence and speckle noise cause phase unwrapping errors that cascade into biased deformation time series and DEM height errors~\citep{massonnet1998radar,zebker1992decorrelation}.

Commercial constellations such as Capella Space operate in high-resolution spotlight mode with per-scene programmable incidence angles, resulting in stacks where incidence angle, perpendicular baseline $B_\perp$, and ground-range resolution vary significantly between acquisitions. Classical filters (Goldstein~\citep{goldstein1998interferometric}, boxcar) treat each interferogram identically, ignoring this geometry diversity. The result is inconsistently filtered phase and elevated triplet-closure errors $\varepsilon_{ijk} = \mathcal{W}(\varphi_{ij} + \varphi_{jk} - \varphi_{ik})$, where $\mathcal{W}(\cdot)$ denotes wrapping to $[-\pi, \pi)$.

Deep learning methods for InSAR denoising have shown promise~\citep{ronneberger2015u}, but supervised approaches require clean reference phases unavailable in practice. The Noise2Noise framework~\citep{lehtinen2018noise2noise} sidesteps this by learning from pairs of independent noisy observations of the same scene. For SAR interferograms, independent noise realisations are obtained by splitting each complex SLC into odd and even spectral sub-looks via FFT masking~\citep{just1994signal}, which share the deterministic phase but have uncorrelated speckle~\citep{lee1994intensity}.

% -----------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Method}

\subsection{Pair-Graph and Geometry}

Given a manifest of $N$ SLC acquisitions, we build a directed pair-graph retaining edges that satisfy: same orbit state (ascending/descending), same look direction, $\Delta t \le 365$\,days, and $|\Delta\theta_{\rm inc}| \le 5^\circ$. Each edge is scored
\begin{equation}
Q_{ij} = \frac{1}{1 + \Delta t_{ij}} \cdot \frac{1}{1 + |\Delta\theta_{{\rm inc},ij}|},
\label{eq:qscore}
\end{equation}
jointly penalising temporal and geometric decorrelation. Perpendicular baseline $B_\perp$ is computed from state vectors at mid-burst time using standard orbit-geometry formulae~\citep{hanssen2001radar}.

\subsection{FiLM-Conditioned U-Net Architecture}

FiLMUNet takes as input the Goldstein-filtered wrapped complex interferogram $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times 2 \times H \times W}$ (real and imaginary channels stacked) and a geometry conditioning vector
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{z} = [\Delta t,\;\theta_{\rm inc},\;\theta_{\rm graze},\;B_\perp,\;m_{\rm mode},\;m_{\rm look},\;{\rm SNR}] \in \mathbb{R}^7.
\end{equation}
A two-layer MLP maps $\mathbf{z} \to \mathbf{c} \in \mathbb{R}^{64}$. The backbone is a four-level encoder-decoder U-Net~\citep{ronneberger2015u} with channel widths $[32, 64, 128, 256]$ and a 512-channel bottleneck. Each level contains two consecutive $3\!\times\!3$ Conv-BN blocks; after the first batch normalisation, Feature-wise Linear Modulation~\citep{perez2018film} rescales the activations:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\mathbf{h}} = \bigl(1 + \boldsymbol{\gamma}(\mathbf{c})\bigr) \odot \mathbf{h} + \boldsymbol{\beta}(\mathbf{c}),
\label{eq:film}
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{\gamma}, \boldsymbol{\beta} \in \mathbb{R}^{C}$ are channel-wise linear projections of $\mathbf{c}$. The residual formulation $(1 + \gamma)$ initialises close to identity. Two output heads produce the denoised interferogram $\hat{\boldsymbol{\varphi}} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times 2 \times H \times W}$ and per-pixel log-variance $\log\hat{\sigma}^2 \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times 1 \times H \times W}$. Total parameters: 7.96\,M.

\subsection{Self-Supervised Training Objective}

\textbf{Sub-look splitting.} Each SLC is split into two independent sub-looks by retaining alternating frequency sub-bands in the range direction via FFT masking~\citep{just1994signal}. From reference and secondary SLCs this yields interferogram pairs $(\varphi^{(1)},\varphi^{(2)})$ sharing the deterministic signal but with independent speckle.

\textbf{Combined loss.}
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L} = \lambda_{\rm n2n}\mathcal{L}_{\rm n2n} + \lambda_{\rm unc}\mathcal{L}_{\rm unc} + \lambda_{\rm cls}\mathcal{L}_{\rm cls} + \lambda_{\rm tmp}\mathcal{L}_{\rm tmp} + \lambda_{\rm grd}\mathcal{L}_{\rm grd},
\label{eq:loss}
\end{equation}
with weights $(1.0,\,0.5,\,0.3,\,0.2,\,0.1)$ respectively.

\noindent$\mathcal{L}_{\rm n2n}$~\citep{lehtinen2018noise2noise}: Noise2Noise L1 on wrapped phase, sub-look~1 as input, sub-look~2 as noisy target:
$\mathcal{L}_{\rm n2n} = \langle|\mathcal{W}(\hat{\varphi} - \varphi^{(2)})|\rangle$.

\noindent$\mathcal{L}_{\rm unc}$: Heteroscedastic negative log-likelihood:
$\tfrac{1}{2}e^{-\log\hat{\sigma}^2} |\mathcal{W}(\hat{\varphi} - \varphi^{(2)})|^2 + \tfrac{1}{2}\log\hat{\sigma}^2$.

\noindent$\mathcal{L}_{\rm cls}$: Triplet-closure consistency:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\rm cls} = \bigl\langle w(p) \bigl(1 - \cos\mathcal{W}(\hat{\varphi}_{ij} + \hat{\varphi}_{jk} - \hat{\varphi}_{ik})\bigr)\bigr\rangle,
\label{eq:closure}
\end{equation}
where $w(p) \!=\! \bar{\gamma}(p)\,/\,(1 + e^{\log\hat{\sigma}^2(p)})$ combines coherence and predicted uncertainty as weights.

\noindent$\mathcal{L}_{\rm tmp}$: SBAS temporal residual $\|W(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}^{\!*} - \hat{\boldsymbol{\varphi}})\|_2^2$ with $\mathbf{x}^{\!*}$ the weighted least-squares solution~\citep{berardino2002sbas}.

\noindent$\mathcal{L}_{\rm grd}$: Fringe-preservation gradient loss on the wrapped phase image, preventing over-smoothing.

\textbf{Training details.} AdamW (${\rm lr}=10^{-4}$, weight decay $10^{-5}$), cosine decay with 2-epoch warm-up, batch size 8, 50 epochs on a single NVIDIA~A100 (40\,GB). Tile size $256\!\times\!256$, stride 128. Seed 42.

\subsection{Uncertainty-Weighted Downstream Processing}

The predicted $\hat{\sigma}^2(p)$ is propagated to two downstream stages. For SNAPHU phase unwrapping~\citep{chen2001two}, Minimum Cost Flow arc costs are proportional to $1/\hat{\sigma}^2(p)$. For SBAS stack inversion~\citep{berardino2002sbas}, the diagonal weight matrix is $\mathbf{W} = {\rm diag}(1/\bar{\sigma}^2_p)$ where $\bar{\sigma}^2_p$ is the spatial median of $\hat{\sigma}^2$ over pair~$p$.
% -----------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Experiments and Results}

\subsection{Dataset}

We use the IEEE GRSS 2026 Data Fusion Contest dataset~\citep{ieeegrss2026dfc}, comprising 791 Capella Space X-band spotlight SLCs spanning 39 geographic AOIs. The primary evaluation site is \textbf{AOI\_000 (Hawaii)}: 221 collects, ascending and descending orbits, incidence angles $35.8^\circ$--$56.3^\circ$, covering active volcanic terrain on the Big Island. After pair-graph filtering, 8{,}834 pairs and 24{,}171 strict triplets are available. We use AOI-based train/val/test splits (${\sim}70/15/15\%$ by collect date) to prevent geographic leakage. For zero-shot transfer, we evaluate on AOI\_008 (Los Angeles, ${\sim}40$ collects) without retraining.

